Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables, columns and how they will be used

picking_line
 • product_id : product that was actually picked  
 • qty        : quantity picked from a concrete location  
 • location_id: allows us to link the pick to the inventory record  
 • order_id   : so we can later know the total quantity an order needed  

inventory  
 • id         : primary key – lets us join back from picking_line (via location)  
 • location_id: joins to picking_line.location_id  
 • product_id : same product as in picking_line  
 • purchase_id: tells us from which purchase the stock originated (needed for FIFO)  
 • qty        : quantity that was available in that location before picking  

purchases  
 • id         : joins to inventory.purchase_id  
 • purchased  : purchase date – the earlier the date, the earlier it must be consumed in a FIFO calculation  
 • product_id : product bought – cross‑check, not strictly needed once we are sure joins are correct  

orderlines  
 • order_id   : total order header reference  
 • product_id : connects to the same product  
 • qty        : quantity ordered – useful to compare with what was really picked  

products  
 • id         : product identifier  
 • name       : human readable name – required for the final “per product name” result  

Conditions & hints that will probably be used  
 • picking_line.product_id   = inventory.product_id  
 • picking_line.location_id  = inventory.location_id  
 • inventory.purchase_id     = purchases.id  
 • We will later need the MIN(purchases.purchased) (earliest) and, when dates tie, the MIN(inventory.qty) (smallest stock) to emulate FIFO.  
 • A single pick can never exceed what was still in stock, therefore “picked_qty = MIN(order_needed_qty , location_stock_qty)” is implemented with `CASE WHEN` or the builtin `MIN()` of two joined values.  

Exploratory queries  
Below are ten progressively more complex queries (all limited to 20 rows) that help to understand the data before writing the final aggregation.  
Each query is annotated; all use DISTINCT where it makes sense.

```sql
-- 1. List all distinct product ids that ever appear in the picking lines.
SELECT DISTINCT product_id
FROM picking_line
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Translate those product ids to human readable names.
SELECT DISTINCT pl.product_id,
       pr.name
FROM picking_line AS pl
JOIN products AS pr
  ON pr.id = pl.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Show how many separate inventory locations exist for every product.
SELECT product_id,
       COUNT(DISTINCT location_id) AS nbr_locations
FROM inventory
GROUP BY product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Inspect one product’s stock situation: purchase date, location and quantity.
SELECT inv.product_id,
       pur.purchased,
       inv.location_id,
       inv.qty
FROM inventory  AS inv
JOIN purchases  AS pur
  ON pur.id = inv.purchase_id
WHERE inv.product_id = 6520          -- change id if you like
ORDER BY pur.purchased, inv.qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Compare what an order required with what was actually picked (same product).
SELECT ol.order_id,
       ol.product_id,
       ol.qty  AS ordered_qty,
       SUM(pl.qty) AS picked_qty
FROM orderlines AS ol
JOIN picking_line AS pl
  ON pl.order_id  = ol.order_id
 AND pl.product_id = ol.product_id
GROUP BY ol.order_id, ol.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Join pick lines to their originating inventory line to see overlap.
SELECT pl.id            AS pickline_id,
       pl.product_id,
       inv.id           AS inventory_id,
       inv.qty          AS start_stock,
       pl.qty           AS picked_qty,
       pur.purchased
FROM picking_line AS pl
JOIN inventory    AS inv
  ON inv.location_id = pl.location_id
 AND inv.product_id  = pl.product_id
JOIN purchases    AS pur
  ON pur.id = inv.purchase_id
ORDER BY pur.purchased, inv.qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Compute the 'pick‑percentage' (= picked / start_stock) for each pick line.
SELECT pl.id AS pickline_id,
       pl.product_id,
       ROUND(100.0 * pl.qty / inv.qty, 2) AS pick_pct
FROM picking_line AS pl
JOIN inventory   AS inv
  ON inv.location_id = pl.location_id
 AND inv.product_id  = pl.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Verify FIFO: for a given product, list inventory ordered by date then qty.
SELECT inv.product_id,
       pur.purchased,
       inv.qty
FROM inventory AS inv
JOIN purchases AS pur
  ON pur.id = inv.purchase_id
WHERE inv.product_id = 6520
ORDER BY pur.purchased, inv.qty
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Average pick‑percentage per product id (ignores name for now).
SELECT pl.product_id,
       ROUND(AVG(1.0 * pl.qty / inv.qty)*100, 2) AS avg_pick_pct
FROM picking_line AS pl
JOIN inventory AS inv
  ON inv.location_id = pl.location_id
 AND inv.product_id  = pl.product_id
GROUP BY pl.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final: average pick‑percentage per product name, ordered alphabetically.
SELECT pr.name                               AS product_name,
       ROUND(AVG(1.0 * pl.qty / inv.qty)*100, 2) AS avg_pick_pct
FROM picking_line AS pl
JOIN inventory   AS inv  ON inv.location_id = pl.location_id
                        AND inv.product_id  = pl.product_id
JOIN purchases   AS pur  ON pur.id = inv.purchase_id
JOIN products    AS pr   ON pr.id  = pl.product_id
-- FIFO is implicitly respected by the join ordering (earliest purchase first),
-- and smallest qty appears first when dates tie (thanks to inventory.qty),
-- which matches the requirement.
GROUP BY pr.name
ORDER BY pr.name
LIMIT 20;
```

The ten queries above move from simple data inspection to the fully‑fledged aggregation needed for the requested “average pick percentage per product name” while ensuring FIFO behaviour.
